@meta
id: 01-07
title: Drill — in, out, saved
chapter: 01
type: drill
skills: cmdline, insert-i, esc, mode-awareness
par: 13
minutes: 5
reps: 8
vscode: click at the start of the line, type, Ctrl-S
open: queue.md

@brief
Eight times: put `- ` at the front of the line, leave insert mode, write
the file with `:w`, then quit with `:q`. Two separate commands, not `:wq`.

You are drilling the colon. By the last rep the `w` and the `q` should
feel like two different decisions, because that is what they are.

@keys
| `i` | insert to the left of the cursor |
| `Esc` | out |
| `:w` | write |
| `:q` | quit |

@hints
The cursor already starts in column one. There is nowhere to move to.
--
`i- ` `Esc` `:w` Enter `:q` Enter. Run the whole thing without looking at
the screen on the last three reps.

@solution
    i - <space> <Esc> :w<Enter> :q<Enter>

Ten keystrokes, and only three of them are the edit.

The reason to drill `:w` on its own is that saving is not the same event
as finishing. A vim session on a file you are actually working on has one
`:q` and dozens of `:w`. Wiring them together in your hands as `:wq` is
how people end up quitting a file they meant to keep open.

If a `:q` ever refuses, look at the message. It means the buffer changed
since the last write, and you either forgot the `:w` or made an edit you
did not notice making.

@start queue.md
Retry the failed payout batch

@expected queue.md
